    <h2>What Is C2PA in Video Files and How Does It Differ from Static Images?</h2>
    <p>
      The Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C2PA) standard establishes an open technical specification for binding cryptographic provenance metadata to digital media. While C2PA assertions in still images are typically embedded inside JPEG APP11 marker segments or PNG custom chunks, video files present a far more complex structural architecture.
    </p>
    <p>
      Video media relies on the ISO Base Media File Format (ISOBMFF, ISO/IEC 14496-12), the foundational container standard underlying MP4, QuickTime MOV, and 3GP formats. In video containers, C2PA manifests are structured as hierarchical JUMBF (JPEG Universal Metadata Box Format, ISO/IEC 19566-5) payloads stored within user-extended box atoms (<code>uuid</code> boxes).
    </p>
    <p>
      Because video files contain gigabytes of temporal audio and video samples divided into independent tracks, C2PA implementations in video can store:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Container-Level Provenance:</strong> A single global manifest embedded inside the movie header (<code>moov</code>) box that documents the generating AI model (such as OpenAI Sora, Google Veo, Runway Gen-3, or Luma Dream Machine) and digital signatures from cloud rendering pipelines.</li>
      <li><strong>Synchronized Metadata Tracks:</strong> Dedicated timed metadata tracks (<code>trak</code> atoms with <code>meta</code> handlers) that record frame-by-frame generative modifications, tracking when AI tools were used to alter specific video segments.</li>
      <li><strong>Assertion Manifests:</strong> Embedded thumbnail previews, cryptographic hashes of video sample tables, and software version history.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      RemoveSynth's video sanitizer dissects the ISOBMFF box hierarchy, excising C2PA manifest boxes without touching the media sample data (<code>mdat</code>), ensuring a 100 percent lossless output.
    </p>

    <h2>The JUMBF Box Hierarchy: Where Content Credentials Hide Inside MP4 and MOV Containers</h2>
    <p>
      To understand how C2PA tracking is dismantled, one must understand how video files organize binary atoms. Every ISOBMFF file consists of serialized boxes, each containing a 4-byte length header and a 4-byte FourCC type code:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><code>ftyp</code> (File Type Box):</strong> Identifies the container brand compatibility (e.g. <code>mp42</code>, <code>isom</code>).</li>
      <li><strong><code>moov</code> (Movie Header Box):</strong> Contains structural metadata describing the video tracks, codecs, frame rates, and sample timing tables. Inside <code>moov</code>, C2PA injectors insert a specialized <code>uuid</code> box whose 16-byte extended type identifier corresponds to the C2PA specification: <code>0xd80072ec-cc1d-11ec-9d64-0242ac120002</code>.</li>
      <li><strong><code>trak</code> (Track Boxes):</strong> Individual audio and video stream descriptions. Some AI editors insert secondary C2PA metadata tracks alongside the primary H.264 or HEVC stream.</li>
      <li><strong><code>mdat</code> (Media Data Box):</strong> Contains the actual compressed video and audio elementary bitstreams (accounting for 99.9 percent of the total file size).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Mathematical Formulation of ISOBMFF Box Traversal and Byte-Offset Adjustment</h2>
    <p>
      An ISOBMFF container file <code>V</code> is modeled as a sequence of serialized box atoms:
    </p>
    <div class="math-block">
      <code>V = [ Box_1, Box_2, ..., Box_K ],  where  Box_i = ( Length_i, Type_i, [ ExtendedLength_i ], Payload_i )</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      When a C2PA manifest box <code>Box_C2PA = ( L_c2pa, 'uuid', UUID_bytes, Manifest_payload )</code> is located within the parent <code>moov</code> container, excision requires updating the parent atom length to maintain structural integrity:
    </p>
    <div class="math-block">
      <code>Length_moov' = Length_moov - L_c2pa</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      Because video sample tables (<code>stco</code> for 32-bit chunk offsets or <code>co64</code> for 64-bit offsets) reference absolute byte positions of media frames within the <code>mdat</code> box, if the <code>moov</code> box precedes <code>mdat</code> (the standard FastStart layout for web streaming), all chunk offsets must be shifted by the excised byte delta:
    </p>
    <div class="math-block">
      <code>Chunk_Offset_j' = Chunk_Offset_j - L_c2pa,  for all  j in [ 1, N_chunks ]</code>
    </div>
    <p>
      This exact mathematical recalculation ensures that video decoders in web browsers and social media platforms stream the video seamlessly without stutter or playback crashes.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked Example: Excising a 48KB C2PA Manifest from a Runway Gen-3 MP4 Video</h2>
    <p>
      To observe the precision of our container sanitization process, consider a real numerical test with an AI-generated video clip:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Source Clip:</strong> High-definition video clip generated by Runway Gen-3 Alpha (<code>sci-fi-drone-shot.mp4</code>)</li>
      <li><strong>Original File Size:</strong> 84,215,840 bytes (80.31 Megabytes)</li>
      <li><strong>Video Codec:</strong> H.264 (High Profile, Level 4.2), 1920 by 1080 pixels at 30.00 fps</li>
      <li><strong>Audio Track:</strong> AAC Stereo, 48 kHz, 192 kbps</li>
      <li><strong>Detected Provenance Payload:</strong> 49,152 bytes (48 KB) JUMBF C2PA manifest inside <code>moov.uuid</code> containing cryptographic signatures and prompt history</li>
      <li><strong>Sanitization Operation:</strong> Excised <code>uuid</code> box, recalculated <code>moov</code> header length, adjusted 420 chunk offsets in <code>stco</code> table, preserved <code>mdat</code> bit-for-bit</li>
      <li><strong>Sanitized Output File Size:</strong> 84,166,688 bytes (80.26 Megabytes)</li>
      <li><strong>Visual Fidelity:</strong> Infinite PSNR (0 video frames re-encoded, 100 percent lossless)</li>
      <li><strong>Processing Time:</strong> 18 milliseconds</li>
    </ul>

